<!-- SHORT: replaces wp_s4_okafor1 -->
<TalkBubble id="wp_s4_okafor1_short" who="Okafor" face="explaining" side="primary">
<Markdown id="wp_s4_okafor1_short_md">You know Ambitious Science Teaching by now, so just the part that matters today. Launch with a phenomenon worth explaining, then draw out what students already think, so their ideas, not the teacher's, drive the unit. The critical and cultural reading adds a question: whose experiences does the phenomenon assume, and whose contributions get taken up? That's what Ms. Boyd's launch is held to today.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<!-- LONG: the original opening line.
<TalkBubble id="wp_s4_okafor1" who="Okafor" face="explaining" side="primary">
<Markdown id="wp_s4_okafor1_md">The framework this course teaches is called Ambitious Science Teaching. Its first set of practices is the one you just learned the vocabulary for: pick a phenomenon worth explaining, launch it, and draw out what students already think, so that their ideas, not the teacher's, drive the lessons that follow. There's also a critical and cultural reading of it, which adds a question: whose experiences does the phenomenon assume, and whose contributions get taken up? That's the reading Ms. Boyd's launch is going to be held to.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>
-->

<TalkBubble id="wp_s4_luke1" who="Luke" face="serious" side="secondary">
<Markdown id="wp_s4_luke1_md">With respect, Dr. Okafor, can I ask something before we start? Is this a framework we're learning, or a conclusion we're supposed to get to? Because the evidence page says nobody's ever run a trial of AST as a whole.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s4_okafor2" who="Okafor" face="calm" side="primary">
<Markdown id="wp_s4_okafor2_md">Both halves of that are true. It's the framework this course teaches, and it hasn't been tested as a package. That's on the evidence page because I put it there. If you think it's wrong, argue it, from the transcript and the research.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s4_nyota1" who="Nyota" face="solemn" side="primary">
<Markdown id="wp_s4_nyota1_md">For the record, I don't buy the second half yet either. Not for Luke's reasons. I'll make my case when we have the transcript in front of us.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<!-- LONG: from here to the end of wp_s4_discourse. The standards/curriculum/
     framework distinction (the "standards behind the plan" crosswalk in s7 and
     in Reference covers that ground now), the vocabulary matching, and the
     conversation after it.
<TalkBubble id="wp_s4_jordan1" who="Jordan" face="cheeky" side="secondary">
<Markdown id="wp_s4_jordan1_md">Okay, I love this already. Two people out before we've even read it.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s4_okafor3" who="Okafor" face="explaining" side="primary">
<Markdown id="wp_s4_okafor3_md">Three words you'll hear used as if they meant the same thing. They don't. A *standard* is like a growth chart: what most kids should be able to do by the end of a grade, and the state tests it. A *curriculum* is a menu: a sequence of units and materials that usually gets a class there. A *framework*, and AST is one, is advice about how to cook. Standards bind. Menus and advice don't, and a teacher who treats them as if they do can't adjust anything for the kid in front of her.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s4_jordan2" who="Jordan" face="serious" side="secondary">
<Markdown id="wp_s4_jordan2_md">I mean, tell that to the pacing guide. And the test in April. Everybody gets measured on the growth chart, so the menu turns into the law about a week after school starts.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<Vertical id="wp_s4_okafor4_and_pointer">
<TalkBubble id="wp_s4_okafor4" who="Okafor" face="calm" side="primary">
<Markdown id="wp_s4_okafor4_md">Yes. That's why she guards her minutes, and later today you'll defend her for it, and you'll be partly right. Keep the difference anyway. A good default menu is a gift; nobody should have to invent breakfast from scratch every morning. But following the guide no matter who's in front of you isn't alignment. It's a treadmill.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>
<Markdown id="wp_s4_std_pointer">
> 📎 The actual Tennessee standards and the national ones, laid out next to Ms. Boyd's plan, are in **Reference › Lesson materials**, under the **Standards behind the plan** tab.
</Markdown>
<ActionButton id="wp_s4_go_materials" label="Open Lesson materials in Reference">
<SetFieldAction id="wp_s4_go_materials_sel" target="wp_course" field="selectedChild" value="wp/wp_ref_materials" />
<SetFieldAction id="wp_s4_go_materials_exp" target="wp_course" field="expandedChapter" value="wp_ref" />
</ActionButton>
</Vertical>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s4_jordan3" who="Jordan" face="cheeky" side="secondary">
<Markdown id="wp_s4_jordan3_md">Also, for the record, I've seen her do the lake thing in person. It's good. Kids love it.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s4_dwayne1" who="Dwayne" face="calm" side="secondary">
<Markdown id="wp_s4_dwayne1_md">I'd like to read it before I decide. I read slow. But first, these question words. I don't have all of them yet.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<Vertical id="wp_s4_discourse">
<Use ref="wp_discourse_match_problem" />
<IntakeGate id="wp_s4_g1" ready="@wp_discourse_match_grader.correct === correctness.correct || @wp_ok_s4.value">
<Markdown id="wp_s4_lock1">🔒 *Get all six matched correctly and the conversation continues.*</Markdown>
<NextReveal id="wp_s4_reveal_b">

<TalkBubble id="wp_s4_dwayne2" who="Dwayne" face="concerned" side="secondary">
<Markdown id="wp_s4_dwayne2_md">Funneling and focusing took me four tries. Is "pressing for reasoning" basically just asking "why" a lot?</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s4_nyota2" who="Nyota" face="solemn" side="primary">
<Markdown id="wp_s4_nyota2_md">Not quite. It's asking a child why *they* think it, so the reason has to come from them. Asking "why?" after you've already given the answer is something else.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s4_luke2" who="Luke" face="serious" side="secondary">
<Markdown id="wp_s4_luke2_md">I'd add one thing. None of these moves work if the kids don't know anything yet. You can't press a nine-year-old for reasoning about why ice floats if nobody's ever taught them what floating depends on.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<Vertical id="wp_s4_okafor5_and_pointer">
<TalkBubble id="wp_s4_okafor5" who="Okafor" face="explaining" side="primary">
<Markdown id="wp_s4_okafor5_md">That's Willingham's argument, and it's on the evidence page. You'll want it later. Speaking of which: the research is in Reference, both sides of it, with the design of every study named. When anyone in this room, including me, says "research shows," ask which study.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>
<Markdown id="wp_s4_evidence_pointer">
> 📎 **Reference › The evidence**: what supports phenomenon-based, responsive science teaching, and what cuts against it.
</Markdown>
</Vertical>

<Vertical id="wp_s4_end">
<TalkBubble id="wp_s4_okafor6" who="Okafor" face="serious" side="primary">
<Markdown id="wp_s4_okafor6_md">One ground rule for the rest of today: nobody says what a move *meant* until they've quoted it.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>
<OnShow id="wp_s4_latch"><SetFieldAction id="wp_s4_latch_set" target="wp_ok_s4" field="value" value="yes" /></OnShow>
</Vertical>

</NextReveal>
