<!-- ===================================================================
     PART 2 — THE PHENOMENON AND THE ARGUMENT
     =================================================================== -->

<Vertical id="wp_s5" title="The phenomenon">
<NextReveal id="wp_s5_reveal">

<Vertical id="wp_s5_scene">
<Markdown id="wp_s5_scene_md">
*Dr. Okafor passes around a printout: one page, the first four minutes of Tuesday's lesson, word for word. Nobody talks while everyone reads.*

**Tuesday, 1:15 p.m., Room 14.** It's the second week back from winter break, and the hard freeze was last week. You were at the back of the room. Read it once all the way through, the way you watched it.
</Markdown>
<Markdown id="wp_s5_materials_pointer">
> 📎 Ms. Boyd's lesson plan, including the answers she expected the children to give, is in **Reference › Lesson materials**. You don't need it yet. You will in a few minutes.
</Markdown>
</Vertical>

<Use ref="wp_transcript_body" />

<Markdown id="wp_s5_shift">
*Pens down. From here on it's the six of you around the table, talking about the transcript you just read.*
</Markdown>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s5_okafor1" who="Okafor" face="calm" side="primary">
<Markdown id="wp_s5_okafor1_md">I'll go first, once, and then it's yours. She opens with a mystery a nine-year-old can't leave alone: how are the fish still alive under there? And she has them turn and talk before anyone answers. Those are real strengths. Don't lose them in what comes next.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s5_jordan1" who="Jordan" face="smile" side="secondary">
<Markdown id="wp_s5_jordan1_md">See, that's what I'm saying. Every kid in that room is talking in the first minute. Half the teachers I had never did that all year.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s5_nyota1" who="Nyota" face="solemn" side="primary">
<Markdown id="wp_s5_nyota1_md">Every kid is talking. And then Marcus talks first. Keep reading.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s5_dwayne1" who="Dwayne" face="calm" side="secondary">
<Markdown id="wp_s5_dwayne1_md">I counted turns. Fourteen for her, one each for four kids, one for the whole class. I don't know yet if that means anything. Writing it down.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<Vertical id="wp_s5_end">
<TalkBubble id="wp_s5_luke1" who="Luke" face="smile" side="secondary">
<Markdown id="wp_s5_luke1_md">Fourteen to four isn't crazy for a launch, honestly. Somebody has to set it up. What I want to know is what she did with the four.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>
<Markdown id="wp_s5_done_md">**I've read the transcript.** (Marking this also opens **Reference › Notes on the transcript**, where you can highlight and annotate it.)</Markdown>
<Done id="wp_launch_read" align="left" label="I've read it" />
<OnShow id="wp_s5_latch"><SetFieldAction id="wp_s5_latch_set" target="wp_ok_s5" field="value" value="yes" /></OnShow>
</Vertical>

</NextReveal>
</Vertical>

<Vertical id="wp_s6" title="Her questions">
<NextReveal id="wp_s6_reveal">

<TalkBubble id="wp_s6_okafor1" who="Okafor" face="explaining" side="primary">
<Markdown id="wp_s6_okafor1_md">She asks eight questions in four minutes, and they don't all do the same job. Sort them.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<Vertical id="wp_s6_questions">
<Collapsible id="wp_s6_transcript" title="The transcript, for reference">
<Use ref="wp_transcript_body" />
</Collapsible>
<Use ref="wp_questions_problem" />
<IntakeGate id="wp_s6_g1" ready="@wp_questions_grader.submitCount &gt; 0 || @wp_ok_s6.value">
<Markdown id="wp_s6_lock1">🔒 *Sort all eight and press **Check**; the conversation continues once you've seen how your sort compares.*</Markdown>
<NextReveal id="wp_s6_reveal_b">

<TalkBubble id="wp_s6_dwayne1" who="Dwayne" face="concerned" side="secondary">
<Markdown id="wp_s6_dwayne1_md">Okay, the freezer one. "What would happen to a bottle of water left in the freezer?" That's a prediction, so I had it as opening up thinking. Then she answered it in the same breath. So which is it?</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<Vertical id="wp_s6_okafor2_and_slides">
<TalkBubble id="wp_s6_okafor2" who="Okafor" face="serious" side="primary">
<Markdown id="wp_s6_okafor2_md">Both, which is why the third column is there: a good question she didn't let them have. Now look at slide six in her deck. The answer is printed right under the question, in red, with no click in between. She didn't run out of time. She built it that way, years ago.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>
<Collapsible id="wp_s6_slides" title="Her slides">
<Use ref="wp_mat_deck_md" />
</Collapsible>
</Vertical>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s6_jordan1" who="Jordan" face="cheeky" side="secondary">
<Markdown id="wp_s6_jordan1_md">To be fair, she had like thirty seconds before the bell on that one.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s6_nyota1" who="Nyota" face="solemn" side="primary">
<Markdown id="wp_s6_nyota1_md">She had four minutes, and she spent one of them on "name the three states of matter."</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s6_luke1" who="Luke" face="serious" side="secondary">
<Markdown id="wp_s6_luke1_md">I'll defend that one, actually. Darnell knew the words, and that's not nothing. Willingham's whole point is you can't think about what you don't know.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s6_okafor3" who="Okafor" face="calm" side="primary">
<Markdown id="wp_s6_okafor3_md">Agreed, it's not nothing. So what did knowing the words let Darnell do next?</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s6_dwayne2" who="Dwayne" face="calm" side="secondary">
<Markdown id="wp_s6_dwayne2_md">Nothing. She moved on. It's right here: "Yes! Solid, liquid, gas," and then the next question.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s6_luke2" who="Luke" face="concerned" side="secondary">
<Markdown id="wp_s6_luke2_md">That's fair. Knowing the words was supposed to be the start of something.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s6_okafor4" who="Okafor" face="explaining" side="primary">
<Markdown id="wp_s6_okafor4_md">Your turn. Choose two of her questions: one that opened up the children's thinking and one that shut it down. For each, quote the question exactly, then explain what it did to how the children were thinking about the science.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<Vertical id="wp_s6_analysis">
<Use ref="wp_discourse_analysis_input" />
<IntakeGate id="wp_s6_g2" ready="wordcount(@wp_discourse_analysis.value) &gt;= 12 || @wp_ok_s6.value">
<Markdown id="wp_s6_lock2"> </Markdown>
<NextReveal id="wp_s6_reveal_c">
<Vertical id="wp_s6_end">
<TalkBubble id="wp_s6_okafor5" who="Okafor" face="serious" side="primary">
<Markdown id="wp_s6_okafor5_md">That's one lens: the science talk. The next one needs her plan open.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>
<OnShow id="wp_s6_latch"><SetFieldAction id="wp_s6_latch_set" target="wp_ok_s6" field="value" value="yes" /></OnShow>
