<Vertical id="wp_s7" title="The plan">
<NextReveal id="wp_s7_reveal">

<Vertical id="wp_s7_sources">
<Markdown id="wp_s7_intro">
## What her plan says

Her unit plan, her lesson plan for Tuesday, and the standards behind them are folded below. Open them; the next few minutes are about one moment, Aaliyah's pipes, read against what she planned.
</Markdown>
<Collapsible id="wp_s7_unit" title="Her unit plan">
<Use ref="wp_mat_unit_md" />
</Collapsible>
<Collapsible id="wp_s7_lesson" title="Her lesson plan, Lesson 1">
<Use ref="wp_mat_lesson_md" />
</Collapsible>
<Collapsible id="wp_s7_standards" title="The standards behind her plan">
<Use ref="wp_std_crosswalk" />
</Collapsible>
</Vertical>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s7_dwayne1" who="Dwayne" face="concerned" side="secondary">
<Markdown id="wp_s7_dwayne1_md">I looked up the codes on her unit plan. 4.PS1.1. Tennessee doesn't have a fourth-grade matter standard; it's third grade and fifth. Am I reading that wrong?</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s7_okafor1" who="Okafor" face="serious" side="primary">
<Markdown id="wp_s7_okafor1_md">You're not. Those codes came off the kit's alignment sheet in 2009, and nobody's checked them since. Honestly, nobody needed to. A standard says what most fourth graders should be able to do by June, not what Room 14 does in January. The unit itself hangs together: it reviews third grade, reaches back to the weathering unit, and builds toward fifth. That last table under "The standards behind her plan" shows how. Today's question is what she did with what a child said.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s7_luke1" who="Luke" face="cheeky" side="secondary">
<Markdown id="wp_s7_luke1_md">Which is kind of my whole problem with "alignment." Half the codes on every plan I've ever seen are decoration.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s7_dwayne2" who="Dwayne" face="calm" side="secondary">
<Markdown id="wp_s7_dwayne2_md">Noted. Writing it down anyway.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s7_nyota1" who="Nyota" face="solemn" side="primary">
<Markdown id="wp_s7_nyota1_md">Then let's read her own plan. Lesson 3 is water expanding when it freezes. Aaliyah said exactly that in Lesson 1, and the class did the same physics with rocks back in November. Nobody in the room said so.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s7_jordan1" who="Jordan" face="serious" side="secondary">
<Markdown id="wp_s7_jordan1_md">And it's the one thing she didn't plan for. Look at her list. "Why does ice float": she expected that, she starred it, it's got a lesson number next to it. Pipes aren't on the list. That's not her being unfair. That's a plan running into a room.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s7_nyota2" who="Nyota" face="solemn" side="primary">
<Markdown id="wp_s7_nyota2_md">I agree with all of that. Now say what it cost Aaliyah.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s7_jordan2" who="Jordan" face="concerned" side="secondary">
<Markdown id="wp_s7_jordan2_md">Ninety seconds she didn't have, if she'd taken it. Last year this exact lesson ran twelve minutes over and she never got to the exit ticket. It's in her notes.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s7_nyota3" who="Nyota" face="solemn" side="primary">
<Markdown id="wp_s7_nyota3_md">One line of marker on the chart paper. "Aaliyah: pipes." Twenty seconds. Bri's question got that. Aaliyah's didn't.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s7_okafor2" who="Okafor" face="explaining" side="primary">
<Markdown id="wp_s7_okafor2_md">Three questions, and answer them from the plan and the transcript. Was the pipe on the unit's path? What would taking it up have cost, in minutes and in anything else? And what did setting it aside cost?</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<Vertical id="wp_s7_analysis">
<Use ref="wp_plan_analysis_input" />
<IntakeGate id="wp_s7_g1" ready="wordcount(@wp_plan_analysis.value) &gt;= 12 || @wp_ok_s7.value">
<Markdown id="wp_s7_lock1"> </Markdown>
<NextReveal id="wp_s7_reveal_b">
<Vertical id="wp_s7_end">
<TalkBubble id="wp_s7_okafor3" who="Okafor" face="serious" side="primary">
<Markdown id="wp_s7_okafor3_md">Jordan and Nyota are both right, and they still disagree. Get used to that. It's most of teaching.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>
<OnShow id="wp_s7_latch"><SetFieldAction id="wp_s7_latch_set" target="wp_ok_s7" field="value" value="yes" /></OnShow>
</Vertical>
</NextReveal>
</IntakeGate>
</Vertical>

</NextReveal>
</Vertical>

<Vertical id="wp_s8" title="Who belongs here?">
<NextReveal id="wp_s8_reveal">

<TalkBubble id="wp_s8_nyota1" who="Nyota" face="solemn" side="primary">
<Markdown id="wp_s8_nyota1_md">I want to say this before anyone else does: I don't agree with the premise of this session. When I was nine I loved Saturn. Saturn was not from my life. That was the whole point of Saturn. If the criticism is that her lake is far away, the criticism is wrong.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s8_luke1" who="Luke" face="smile" side="secondary">
<Markdown id="wp_s8_luke1_md">Okay, I did not expect to agree with Nyota this early, but yes. Kids deserve windows, not just mirrors. A kid in Memphis doesn't only get to care about Memphis.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s8_nyota2" who="Nyota" face="solemn" side="primary">
<Markdown id="wp_s8_nyota2_md">We don't agree, Luke. We got to the same sentence from different places.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s8_jordan1" who="Jordan" face="serious" side="secondary">
<Markdown id="wp_s8_jordan1_md">And that's not even the criticism I'd make. My thing is, she did fine. She got ideas out, she had them talk, and Aaliyah's story was going to eat the lesson. Y'all haven't had twenty-two nine-year-olds at 1:15. That story takes them for the rest of the hour.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s8_okafor1" who="Okafor" face="explaining" side="primary">
<Markdown id="wp_s8_okafor1_md">You might all be right. Jordan has run a room, so listen to him about the room. But argue it from the transcript. Find the lines where the launch told the children something about whose experience counts here. Then we'll see whether it's the lake or something else.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<Vertical id="wp_s8_moments">
<Use ref="wp_equity_moments" />
<IntakeGate id="wp_s8_g1" ready="@wp_equity_moments_grader.submitCount &gt; 0 || @wp_ok_s8.value">
<Markdown id="wp_s8_lock1">🔒 *Mark her lines and press **Check**; the conversation continues once you've seen the feedback.*</Markdown>
<NextReveal id="wp_s8_reveal_b">

<TalkBubble id="wp_s8_nyota3" who="Nyota" face="solemn" side="primary">
<Markdown id="wp_s8_nyota3_md">Okay, then here's the transcript. The lake is four lines. What she does with Aaliyah is one line, and it's worse than the lake. The pipe was the same science. She sent it back to its seat.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s8_jordan2" who="Jordan" face="concerned" side="secondary">
<Markdown id="wp_s8_jordan2_md">Yeah. No, that one I can't defend on the science. I can defend it on the room.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s8_nyota4" who="Nyota" face="solemn" side="primary">
<Markdown id="wp_s8_nyota4_md">Then say that. Say she was worried about losing the room. Don't say the pipe was off topic.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s8_dwayne1" who="Dwayne" face="concerned" side="secondary">
<Markdown id="wp_s8_dwayne1_md">Can I ask the dumb one? Diego. Y'all keep saying "ways of knowing." I hear that as "ways of showing you know." He didn't get one. Is that what it means, or am I off?</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s8_okafor2" who="Okafor" face="serious" side="primary">
<Markdown id="wp_s8_okafor2_md">You're not off. That's closer than the framework's own definition. He was asked to perform knowing: in front of the room, in his second language, in the fastest minute of the lesson. Then he was passed over in about three seconds. And her plan says, in her own handwriting, don't cold-call Diego. She did it anyway, with the clock running.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s8_luke2" who="Luke" face="concerned" side="secondary">
<Markdown id="wp_s8_luke2_md">That's the part I can't get past. I'll be honest, I don't love "whose experiences count." To me every kid's experience counts or none of them do. But that's exactly why Diego bothers me. Forget groups. That's one kid, in front of everybody, and her own plan told her not to.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s8_okafor3" who="Okafor" face="calm" side="primary">
<Markdown id="wp_s8_okafor3_md">Then you, Nyota, and I agree about Diego, and we can argue about the rest. Let me add something from outside this country. When I was in primary school in Enugu, the "relevance" question wasn't about phenomena at all. It was about language. National policy said the first years should be taught in the language of the community, which for us was Igbo. Most schools taught in English anyway, because the exams were in English and the exams were the door out. Nobody there called that culturally responsive teaching. Diego's question is a language question first.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s8_nyota5" who="Nyota" face="solemn" side="primary">
<Markdown id="wp_s8_nyota5_md">In the camp in Tanzania, we were taught the Congolese curriculum, in French, for an exam in a country most of us had never seen. Nobody wanted lessons about the camp. The lessons about our lives were the ones that told us we'd be staying. Amani, in Room 14, was born in a camp like mine, twenty years later. I'd like to know what he wants before anyone decides it's pipes.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s8_jordan3" who="Jordan" face="serious" side="secondary">
<Markdown id="wp_s8_jordan3_md">Okay. That's... yeah. I'd have to sit with that.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<Vertical id="wp_s8_outside_pointer">
<Markdown id="wp_s8_outside_md">
> 📎 More on what "culturally responsive" can mean outside the U.S. (Nigeria, Tanzania, refugee education, Aotearoa New Zealand) is in **Reference › Lesson materials**, under the **Frameworks** tab.
</Markdown>
</Vertical>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s8_okafor4" who="Okafor" face="explaining" side="primary">
<Markdown id="wp_s8_okafor4_md">Now answer Jordan directly. Whose experiences does the launch assume? What did it do with the ones it didn't assume? And what did that tell the room about who does science here? Argue it from the transcript, not from how you feel about any of us.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<Vertical id="wp_s8_analysis">
<Use ref="wp_equity_analysis_input" />
<IntakeGate id="wp_s8_g2" ready="wordcount(@wp_equity_analysis.value) &gt;= 12 || @wp_ok_s8.value">
<Markdown id="wp_s8_lock2"> </Markdown>
<NextReveal id="wp_s8_reveal_c">
<Vertical id="wp_s8_end">
<TalkBubble id="wp_s8_okafor5" who="Okafor" face="serious" side="primary">
<Markdown id="wp_s8_okafor5_md">Whatever you concluded, you argued it from the transcript. Keep that habit.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>
<OnShow id="wp_s8_latch"><SetFieldAction id="wp_s8_latch_set" target="wp_ok_s8" field="value" value="yes" /></OnShow>
</Vertical>
