<!-- ===================================================================
     PART 4 — YOUR OWN LAUNCH (items 5 and 17 of the September meeting notes)
     DRAFT for Wesam Salem's revision. This screen is a lesson plan in the
     shape of Ms. Boyd's own (objective, standards, timeline, anticipated
     responses), plus targeted questions about the phenomenon and about
     culturally responsive teaching for specific children in Room 14. It is a
     form, not a conversation: candidates move around it and revise. The
     checklist at the bottom holds the last screen until the core is done.
     =================================================================== -->

<Vertical id="wp_s12" title="Plan your own launch">

<Markdown id="wp_s12_intro">
## Plan your own launch

**Same room, same science, your forty-five minutes.** Ms. Boyd has agreed to let you teach a launch of the same science to Room 14 next week, in her usual period. Keep the science: water expands when it freezes, so ice floats, a full bottle cracks, a pipe bursts, and a rock splits along a wet crack (the weathering this class studied in November). Everything else is yours to decide.

Write it as a plan a substitute could run, not as an essay. You can move around this page and revise anything. The checklist at the bottom shows what's needed before Dr. Okafor reads it.
</Markdown>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s12_okafor1" who="Okafor" face="explaining" side="primary">
<Markdown id="wp_s12_okafor1_md">Pick your anchor first. Then plan it the way she did, in blocks of time. Say what you expect the children to say, including at least two things her list didn't have. And for three particular children, tell me what they'll actually do, not what you'll say about them.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<Collapsible id="wp_s12_refs" title="Open her plan, the roster, the standards, and the science while you work">
<Collapsible id="wp_s12_ref_target" title="The science, at a fourth-grade level">
<Use ref="wp_science_target" />
</Collapsible>
<Collapsible id="wp_s12_ref_lesson" title="Her Lesson 1 plan, for comparison">
<Use ref="wp_mat_lesson_md" />
</Collapsible>
<Collapsible id="wp_s12_ref_roster" title="The children in Room 14">
<Use ref="wp_room14_kids" />
</Collapsible>
<Collapsible id="wp_s12_ref_standards" title="The standards behind the unit">
<Use ref="wp_std_crosswalk" />
</Collapsible>
</Collapsible>

<!-- 1. Anchor -->
<Markdown id="wp_s12_h1">### 1. Your anchor</Markdown>
<Use ref="wp_anchor_choice" />
<UseDynamic id="wp_redesign_branch" targetRef="wp_anchor_choice" when="@wp_anchor_choice.value" />

<TalkBubble id="wp_s12_jordan1" who="Jordan" face="cheeky" when="@wp_anchor_choice.value === 'wp_branch_memphis_freeze'" side="secondary">
<Markdown id="wp_s12_jordan1_md">Same, I'm taking the freeze too. It's the obvious one. Nyota's about to say it's the easy way out.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s12_nyota1" who="Nyota" face="solemn" when="@wp_anchor_choice.value === 'wp_branch_memphis_freeze'" side="primary">
<Markdown id="wp_s12_nyota1_md">It is the easy way out. It's also correct. Those aren't opposites. Just remember Thuy still doesn't have hot water. Decide before Tuesday what you'll do when a child tells you something like that.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s12_nyota2" who="Nyota" face="solemn" when="@wp_anchor_choice.value === 'wp_branch_far'" side="primary">
<Markdown id="wp_s12_nyota2_md">I'm doing ice cores from Antarctica. I know what you're going to ask, and I don't know yet how a child who's never seen ice outside a freezer gets in. I'd rather say that than pretend.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s12_dwayne1" who="Dwayne" face="calm" when="@wp_anchor_choice.value === 'wp_branch_own_phenomenon'" side="secondary">
<Markdown id="wp_s12_dwayne1_md">I'm doing the river at Meeman-Shelby. And I'm going to find out who's never been before I plan it, not after.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble id="wp_s12_luke1" who="Luke" face="smile" when="@wp_anchor_choice.value === 'wp_branch_lake_reframed'" side="secondary">
<Markdown id="wp_s12_luke1_md">Lake for me too. But I'm putting a jar of water in the freezer in front of them on Monday, so on Tuesday every kid in the room has seen ice do something, not just Marcus. Then the lake. Guided, not a guessing game.</Markdown>
</TalkBubble>

<!-- 2. Standards -->
<Markdown id="wp_s12_h2">
### 2. Which standards does your launch serve?

Check every one your launch is really working toward. Look back at the crosswalk if you need to; a launch usually serves one or two well, not all of them.
</Markdown>
<CheckboxInput id="wp_plan_standards">
  <Key id="wp_ps_tn3" value="tn_3ps11">Tennessee 3.PS1.1: a model of solids, liquids, and gases made of particles too small to see</Key>
  <Key id="wp_ps_tn4" value="tn_4ess21">Tennessee 4.ESS2.1: mechanical weathering, including frost wedging</Key>
  <Key id="wp_ps_tn5" value="tn_5ps11">Tennessee 5.PS1.1: explaining phase changes from observations and measurements</Key>
  <Key id="wp_ps_ng2" value="ngss_2ps14">NGSS 2-PS1-4: some changes caused by heating or cooling can be reversed, and some cannot</Key>
  <Key id="wp_ps_ng5" value="ngss_5ps11">NGSS 5-PS1-1: matter is made of particles too small to be seen</Key>
  <Key id="wp_ps_ng4" value="ngss_4ess21">NGSS 4-ESS2-1: evidence of weathering by water, ice, wind, or vegetation</Key>
</CheckboxInput>

<!-- 3. Objective and the phenomenon -->
<Markdown id="wp_s12_h3">### 3. The objective and the phenomenon</Markdown>
<Markdown id="wp_s12_objective_md">**Your objective,** written the way Ms. Boyd wrote hers, for the children to read on the board.</Markdown>
<LineInput id="wp_plan_objective" placeholder="I can..." />
<Markdown id="wp_s12_question_md">**The exact question you'll open with.** This is the question the whole unit is going to work on.</Markdown>
<LineInput id="wp_plan_question" placeholder="The question you'll pose to launch the phenomenon" />
<Markdown id="wp_s12_puzzle_md">**What makes it puzzling?** What will the children in Room 14 expect to happen, and what happens instead? How will you keep that question open past minute ten instead of answering it?</Markdown>
<TextArea id="wp_plan_puzzle" rows="3" placeholder="They'll expect... Instead... I'll keep it open by..." />

<!-- 4. Timeline -->
<Markdown id="wp_s12_h4">
### 4. Your forty-five minutes

The same blocks Ms. Boyd used, so you can compare. For each block: what happens, and what you say or ask. A line or two is enough.
</Markdown>
<Markdown id="wp_s12_t1_md">**0–5 min**</Markdown>
<TextArea id="wp_plan_t1" rows="2" placeholder="What happens, and what you say or ask" />
<Markdown id="wp_s12_t2_md">**5–10 min**</Markdown>
<TextArea id="wp_plan_t2" rows="2" placeholder="What happens, and what you say or ask" />
<Markdown id="wp_s12_t3_md">**10–20 min**</Markdown>
<TextArea id="wp_plan_t3" rows="2" placeholder="What happens, and what you say or ask" />
<Markdown id="wp_s12_t4_md">**20–30 min**</Markdown>
<TextArea id="wp_plan_t4" rows="2" placeholder="What happens, and what you say or ask" />
<Markdown id="wp_s12_t5_md">**30–40 min**</Markdown>
<TextArea id="wp_plan_t5" rows="2" placeholder="What happens, and what you say or ask" />
<Markdown id="wp_s12_t6_md">**40–45 min**</Markdown>
<TextArea id="wp_plan_t6" rows="2" placeholder="What happens, and what you say or ask" />

<!-- 5. Anticipated responses -->
<Markdown id="wp_s12_h5">
### 5. What you expect them to say, and what you'll do

Three things a child might say, and your move for each. At least two should be things Ms. Boyd's list didn't have: think of Aaliyah's pipe, Jaylen's rock candy, Keisha's salt, Marcus's "the cold comes from the sky."
</Markdown>
<Markdown id="wp_s12_a1_md">**A child might say:**</Markdown>
<LineInput id="wp_plan_ant1" placeholder="What a child might say" />
<Markdown id="wp_s12_m1_md">**Your move:**</Markdown>
<LineInput id="wp_plan_move1" placeholder="What you'll do or say next" />
<Markdown id="wp_s12_a2_md">**A child might say:**</Markdown>
<LineInput id="wp_plan_ant2" placeholder="What a child might say" />
<Markdown id="wp_s12_m2_md">**Your move:**</Markdown>
<LineInput id="wp_plan_move2" placeholder="What you'll do or say next" />
<Markdown id="wp_s12_a3_md">**A child might say:**</Markdown>
<LineInput id="wp_plan_ant3" placeholder="What a child might say" />
<Markdown id="wp_s12_m3_md">**Your move:**</Markdown>
<LineInput id="wp_plan_move3" placeholder="What you'll do or say next" />

<!-- 6. Who gets in, and how (targeted questions on culturally responsive teaching and access) -->
<Markdown id="wp_s12_h6">
### 6. Who gets in, and how

Three children from the roster. For each, describe what they will *do* in your launch, not what you'll say about them.
</Markdown>
<Markdown id="wp_s12_diego_md">**Diego.** Fourteen months from Honduras; WIDA composite 2.1, with listening and speaking ahead of reading and writing; reads Spanish comfortably; fastest in the room at multi-digit subtraction; has seen ice only in a cup. What does he do in your first ten minutes?</Markdown>
<TextArea id="wp_plan_diego" rows="3" placeholder="In the first ten minutes, Diego will..." />
<Markdown id="wp_s12_kenny_md">**Kenny.** IEP for a reading disability; talks his way through science better than he writes it; pulled out for intervention two of the three science periods a week, so he may miss the lesson after yours. How does he get in, and how will he catch up?</Markdown>
<TextArea id="wp_plan_kenny" rows="3" placeholder="Kenny will... If he misses the next lesson..." />
<Markdown id="wp_s12_home_md">**Knowledge from home.** Whose knowledge from home or from the community will you invite, and how will you take it up instead of setting it aside? And what will you do if a child tells you something painful, the way Thuy did in the replay ("we still don't got hot water")?</Markdown>
<TextArea id="wp_plan_home" rows="3" placeholder="I'll invite... I'll take it up by... If a child shares something painful, I'll..." />

<!-- 7. Off the path -->
<Markdown id="wp_s12_h7">
### 7. When it goes off the path

A child says something interesting that isn't on the path. How will you tell an idea that belongs on the chart today (Keisha's salt) from one that belongs to a different unit (Jaylen's rock candy), and what will you do with each?
</Markdown>
<TextArea id="wp_plan_offpath" rows="3" placeholder="When a child says something interesting and off the path, I will..." />

<!-- 8. Exit ticket -->
<Markdown id="wp_s12_h8">
### 8. How you'll know what they think

What will you collect at the end, and what will it tell you about each child's thinking that you can use in the next lesson?
</Markdown>
<TextArea id="wp_plan_exit" rows="3" placeholder="At the end I'll collect... It will tell me..." />

<!-- 9. Make your case -->
<Markdown id="wp_s12_h9">
### 9. Make your case

Why is your launch scientifically rigorous, culturally relevant, and accessible to every learner in Room 14?
</Markdown>
<Use ref="wp_redesign_justify_input" />

