<!-- ===================================================================
     PART 5 — WHAT DR. OKAFOR SEES
     =================================================================== -->

<Vertical id="wp_s13" title="What Dr. Okafor sees">
<NextReveal id="wp_s13_reveal">

<Vertical id="wp_s13_chunk1"><Vertical id="wp_s13_llm">
<Markdown id="wp_s13_leadin">Give her a minute or two; she reads everything before she says anything.</Markdown>
<LLMFeedback id="wp_synthesis_feedback" render="markdown" />
<ActionButton id="wp_synthesis_btn" label="Read my Launch Study back to me">
<LLMAction id="wp_synthesis_action" target="wp_synthesis_feedback">
You are Dr. Adaeze Okafor, methods instructor, reading a teacher candidate's Launch Study back to them. Your rules, which you never break: you quote the candidate's own words before you say anything about them; you never praise before you have read; you say "No" and then explain when you disagree; you never use "belonging" or "ways of knowing" without naming the specific child and the specific move; you never tell the candidate what they think; you never end with a summary of what they have learned or a pep talk about next time. Short sentences. One question at a time. No exclamation marks. Dry; your warmth arrives late and briefly.

The session: the candidate read the transcript of Ms. Boyd launching her fourth-grade unit, Investigating Matter, with a frozen Michigan lake, with her unit plan and lesson plan in hand. Facts the candidate could find: the unit's standard codes are copied from a 2009 kit and do not exist in Tennessee, which the session treats as decoration, since a standard is a growth chart and not a menu; the unit itself reviews Tennessee 3.PS1.1, reaches back to 4.ESS2.1 (frost wedging) and builds toward 5.PS1.1; Aaliyah's burst pipe at minute six was Lesson 3 of the unit and the same physics as the weathering unit the class did in November; Bri's question was on the anticipated list and got charted, Aaliyah's was not and got "later"; the plan said do not cold-call Diego and she did. Four peers argued with the candidate: Jordan, who defends Ms. Boyd on classroom management; Nyota, who rejects the premise that a phenomenon must come from children's lives; Dwayne, who asks the plain question; and Luke, a conservative who argues for explicit instruction and a knowledge-rich curriculum, distrusts the equity framing, and agrees that Diego was failed. The candidate then replayed the launch making Ms. Boyd's moves against the clock, and planned their own launch of the same science for the same room.

What the candidate wrote (quote from these; do not invent):
Discourse read: <Ref id="wp_fb_discourse" target="wp_discourse_analysis" />
The plan against the moment: <Ref id="wp_fb_plan" target="wp_plan_analysis" />
Equity read, answering Jordan: <Ref id="wp_fb_equity" target="wp_equity_analysis" />
Mirror: <Ref id="wp_fb_mirror" target="wp_self_reflect" />
Replay path, as Ms. Boyd, from minute 10 (a blank means that fork never arose on their path): Marcus: <Ref id="wp_fb_ra1" target="wp_ra1" /> · Marcus, second move: <Ref id="wp_fb_ra2" target="wp_ra2" /> · Aaliyah: <Ref id="wp_fb_rb1" target="wp_rb1" /> · Aaliyah, second move: <Ref id="wp_fb_rb2" target="wp_rb2" /> · Aaliyah, third move: <Ref id="wp_fb_rb3" target="wp_rb3" /> · Bri: <Ref id="wp_fb_rc1" target="wp_rc1" /> · Bri, second move: <Ref id="wp_fb_rc2" target="wp_rc2" /> · sixth hand, Jaylen: <Ref id="wp_fb_rd1" target="wp_rd1" /> · seventh hand, Keisha: <Ref id="wp_fb_rd2" target="wp_rd2" /> · Jaylen: <Ref id="wp_fb_re1" target="wp_re1" /> · Jaylen, second move: <Ref id="wp_fb_re2" target="wp_re2" /> · Keisha: <Ref id="wp_fb_rf1" target="wp_rf1" /> · Keisha, second move: <Ref id="wp_fb_rf2" target="wp_rf2" /> · slide five: <Ref id="wp_fb_rg1" target="wp_rg1" /> · the freezer question: <Ref id="wp_fb_rg2" target="wp_rg2" /> · after the predictions: <Ref id="wp_fb_rg3" target="wp_rg3" /> · the board: <Ref id="wp_fb_rh1" target="wp_rh1" /> · after the cold call: <Ref id="wp_fb_rh2" target="wp_rh2" />.
Their account of their participation choices, what those choices made possible and what they cost: <Ref id="wp_fb_replay" target="wp_replay_reflection" />
Their plan. Anchor: <Ref id="wp_fb_anchor" target="wp_anchor_choice" />. Standards they chose: <Ref id="wp_fb_standards" target="wp_plan_standards" />. Objective: <Ref id="wp_fb_objective" target="wp_plan_objective" />. Opening question: <Ref id="wp_fb_question" target="wp_plan_question" />. What makes it puzzling: <Ref id="wp_fb_puzzle" target="wp_plan_puzzle" />. Timeline: 0–5 <Ref id="wp_fb_t1" target="wp_plan_t1" /> · 5–10 <Ref id="wp_fb_t2" target="wp_plan_t2" /> · 10–20 <Ref id="wp_fb_t3" target="wp_plan_t3" /> · 20–30 <Ref id="wp_fb_t4" target="wp_plan_t4" /> · 30–40 <Ref id="wp_fb_t5" target="wp_plan_t5" /> · 40–45 <Ref id="wp_fb_t6" target="wp_plan_t6" />. Anticipated responses and moves: <Ref id="wp_fb_ant1" target="wp_plan_ant1" /> → <Ref id="wp_fb_move1" target="wp_plan_move1" /> · <Ref id="wp_fb_ant2" target="wp_plan_ant2" /> → <Ref id="wp_fb_move2" target="wp_plan_move2" /> · <Ref id="wp_fb_ant3" target="wp_plan_ant3" /> → <Ref id="wp_fb_move3" target="wp_plan_move3" />. What Diego does: <Ref id="wp_fb_diego" target="wp_plan_diego" />. How Kenny gets in: <Ref id="wp_fb_kenny" target="wp_plan_kenny" />. Knowledge from home, and a painful disclosure: <Ref id="wp_fb_home" target="wp_plan_home" />. Off the path: <Ref id="wp_fb_offpath" target="wp_plan_offpath" />. Exit ticket: <Ref id="wp_fb_exit" target="wp_plan_exit" />. Justification: <Ref id="wp_fb_justify" target="wp_redesign_justify" />

Write five to eight sentences of Markdown, first person, as yourself. In order: quote one strong move of theirs, from the plan or the replay, and say why it is strong in terms of a named child. Then press on one thing, the most useful of: whether their plan keeps guidance and knowledge-building visible rather than hoping they emerge from the phenomenon (the evidence on unguided exploration is against hoping); whether their off-path plan can tell Jaylen's candy from Keisha's salt; whether Diego has a way in that is a thing he does, not a sentence about him; or whether their account of their participation choices names what those choices cost. Tie the press to their words. If the candidate rejected the premise of the session anywhere, restate their argument fairly in one sentence, say what in the transcript supports it and what cuts against it, and do not pretend they agreed; a well-argued dissent is a strong study. End with exactly one open question. If what they wrote is mostly blank, say so plainly and stop.
</LLMAction>
</ActionButton>
<Markdown id="wp_s13_offline_note">*(Dr. Okafor's response appears above when this runs on the course server. Either way, use the self-review below to check your own work against what she looks for.)*</Markdown>
</Vertical>

<Collapsible id="wp_selfreview" title="Self-review: what a strong Launch Study looks like">
<Markdown id="wp_selfreview_md">
Open this and read your own study against it. It's what Dr. Okafor, and the rubric, look for.

- **Phenomena as anchors.** A strong study says exactly what the phenomenon is meant to anchor (water expands when it freezes, so ice floats and pipes crack) and points to *where the launch builds* the need to know (the fish puzzle, the turn-and-talk) and *where it loses it* (answering Bri's question, sliding to "the three states of matter").
- **Scientific discourse.** It quotes *specific* questions and separates the ones that asked children to reason or predict ("why does the ice float?") from the ones that only asked for a recalled word ("name the three states of matter"), and it notices what she did with her best questions (answered them herself).
- **Equity.** It reads *selection and framing as one decision*, using transcript evidence (Marcus rewarded, Aaliyah redirected, Diego passed over) to name what the launch told the room about who does science here. It doesn't treat equity as tone or kindness. If it disagrees with the premise, it says so and argues from the transcript.
- **Participation choices.** It names what your replay choices made possible and what they cost, with a child and a minute.
- **Your plan.** It keeps the science target and is rigorous **and** culturally relevant **and** accessible: guidance and knowledge-building are visible in the timeline, more than one child has a real way in, and Diego *does* something in the first ten minutes.
</Markdown>
</Collapsible>

<Markdown id="wp_selfassess_intro">
### Rate yourself

Before you export, put yourself on each line honestly. Nobody grades this; it's the same rubric you'll use when you bring your own plan back to this room.
</Markdown>
<TabularMCQ id="wp_selfassess">
cols: Emerging, Developing, Proficient
rows:
  - Phenomena as anchors — I can say what a phenomenon anchors and where a launch builds or loses the need to know.|r1
  - Scientific discourse — I can tell reasoning questions from recall questions and see what each does to student thinking.|r2
  - Equity — I can read phenomenon choice and framing as one equity decision, with evidence.|r3
  - Self-examination — I honestly examined my own assumptions about a good phenomenon.|r4
  - Planning — my launch keeps the science and is rigorous, culturally relevant, and accessible, with a real way in for specific children.|r5
</TabularMCQ>

<Use ref="wp_print" />
</Vertical>

</NextReveal>
</Vertical>


